<section class="mp-market-explainer" aria-labelledby="over-explainer-heading">
    <h2 id="over-explainer-heading">How Over 2.5 Goals picks are built</h2>
    <p>
        Over 2.5 Goals is a totals market: the model is publishing that the match is more likely to
        finish with three or more goals than with two or fewer. It does not say which team scores,
        whether both teams score, or who wins. That focus makes Over useful when both sides create
        chances — and fragile when a match turns into a cagey midfield battle after an early red card
        or a weather delay.
    </p>

    <h3>What appears on today’s Over card</h3>
    <p>
        Published overs are fixtures whose calibrated Over probability cleared the live Over 2.5
        threshold. Each card shows league, teams, kickoff, confidence, and status. Filters help you
        scan early vs late kickoffs, isolate a league, or keep only strong/elite confidence bands.
        Finished rows show the settled score so you can see whether the over landed without leaving
        the page.
    </p>

    <h3>Signals behind an Over pick</h3>
    <p>
        Totals draw heavily on the Dixon-Coles score matrix (attack and defence strengths with time
        decay), Elo context for recent form and home advantage, de-vigged bookmaker Over/Under prices,
        and a third-party probability feed when quality checks pass. After blending in logit space,
        Under is treated as the complement of Over so the two-way market stays coherent. Calibration
        then reshapes the raw blend so stated percentages track long-run frequencies more honestly.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>High Over confidence often coincides with open leagues or attack-heavy matchups.</li>
        <li>A published Over can still fail at 2–0, 1–1, or 2–0 after a late miss.</li>
        <li>Live scores update during the match; final settlement uses the full-time total.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Reading confidence on totals</h3>
    <p>
        A 70% Over pick means the calibrated model assigns roughly seven-in-ten chance to three-plus
        goals — which still implies three misses in ten over a large sample. Do not confuse confidence
        with “number of goals expected.” The market is binary at the 2.5 line. For how thresholds and
        calibrators are promoted, see <a href="/methodology">Methodology</a>. For honest recent
        outcomes, open <a href="/results">Results</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Empty days and schedule effects</h3>
    <p>
        Some days simply lack enough fixtures that clear the Over threshold. Defensive leagues,
        cup ties with rotated squads, or sparse midweeks can produce a short list. That is a feature
        of threshold publishing, not a missing scrape. Come back after the next generation window,
        or review curated multi-leg lists on <a href="/betslips">Betslips</a> when those runs complete.
    </p>

    <h3>Responsible use</h3>
    <p>
        Totals markets swing on a single late goal. Use this card as an informational filter, not as
        a promise of profit. The site is 18+ and not a bookmaker. Details:
        <a href="/Privacy#responsible-use">responsible use</a>, <a href="/faq">FAQ</a>.
    </p>
</section>
